'''3.''' Increase by something added; that which is added; augmentation; addition: as, an ''accession'' of wealth, territory, or numbers.
{{smaller block|The only ''accession'' which the Roman Empire received was the province of Britain.
{{right|''Gibbon.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The yule log drew an unusually large ''accession'' of guests around the Christmas hearth.
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, I. 17.}}}}
{{smaller block|The ship brought but twenty passengers, and quenched all hope of immediate ''accessions.''
{{right|''Bancroft'', Hist. U. S., I. 285.}}}}
'''4.''' In ''law'', a mode of acquiring property, by which the owner of a corporeal substance which receives an addition by growth or by the application of labor has a right to the thing added or to the improvement, as an addition to a house made by a tenant under an ordinary lease.—'''5.''' In ''med.'', the attack, approach, or commencement of a disease; access.—'''6.''' In the election of a pope, the transference of votes from one candidate to another, when the scrutiny has not resulted in a choice. The opportunity of doing this is called an ''accessit'' (which see).—{{smaller|'''Deed of accession,''' in ''Scots law'', a deed executed by the creditors of a bankrupt, by which they approve of a trust given by their debtor for the general behoof, and bind themselves to concur in the plans proposed for extricating his affairs.='''Syn. 2.''' Consent, compliance, assent, acquiescence.—'''3.''' Increase, addition, increment, extension, augmentation.}}

'''accessional''' (ak-sesh′ọn-al), ''a.'' [=Pg. ''accessional'', < L. as if *''accessionalis'': see ''accession.''] Consisting in or due to accession; giving increase or enlargement; additional.

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|The specific and ''accessional'' perfections which the human understanding derives from it. {{right|''Coleridge.''}}}}
{{smaller block|I regard that, rather, as a superinduced, collateral, ''accessional'' fame, a necessity of greatness.
{{right|''R. Choate'', Addresses, p. 522.}}}}

'''accessit''' (ak-ses′it), ''n.'' [L., he has come near, 3d pers. sing. perf. ind. of ''accedere'', to come to or near: see ''accede.''] '''1.''' In English and other colleges, a certificate or prize awarded to a student of second (or lower) merit: as, second ''accessit'', third, fourth, etc., ''accessit.''—'''2.''' In the election of a pope, an opportunity given the members of the conclave, after each ballot, to revise their votes.
{{smaller block|Every morning a ballot is cast, followed in the evening by an “''accessit''”; that is, if the morning ballot has led to no result, any of the electors is allowed to transfer his vote to that one of the candidates whom he can expect thereby to get elected. {{right|''Schaff-Herzog'', Encyc., I. 521.}}}}

'''accessive†''' (ak-ses′iv), ''a.'' [< ML. ''accessivus'' (rare, and special sense uncertain, but lit. ‘additional’), < L. ''accessus'', addition: see ''access.''] Additional; contributory.
{{smaller block|God “opened the eyes of one that was born blind” and had increased this cæeity by his own ''accessive'' and excessive wickedness. {{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, II. 379.}}}}

'''accessorial''' (ak-se-sō′ri-a̤l), ''a.'' Pertaining to an accessory: as, ''accessorial'' agency.
{{smaller block|Mere ''accessorial'' guilt was not enough to convict him.
{{right|''R. Choate'', Addresses, p. 265.}}}}

'''accessorily''' (ak-ses′o̤-ri-li or ak′se-sō-ri-li), ''adv.'' In the manner of an accessory; not as principal, but as a subordinate agent. Also written ''accessarily.''

readings.c2s1:
''accessory'' buds are developed by the side of or above the normal axillary bud.—{{smaller|'''Accessory action,''' in ''Scots law,'' an action in some degree subservient or ancillary to another action.—'''Accessory contract,''' one made for the purpose of assuring the performance of a prior contract, either by the same parties or by others, such as a suretyship, a mortgage, or a pledge. ''Bouvier.''—'''Accessory disk,''' the thin, slightly dim, and anisotropous disk seen near the intermediate disk in certain forms and conditions of striated muscle-fibers.—'''Accessory fruits,''' those fruits a considerable portion of whose substance is distinct from the seed-vessel and formed of the accrescent and succulent calyx, or torus, or receptacle, bracts, etc.—'''Accessory muscles.''' See ''accessorius.''—'''Accessory obligation,''' an obligation incidental or subordinate to another obligation. Thus, an obligation for the regular payment of interest is ''accessory'' to the obligation to pay the principal; a mortgage to secure payment of a bond is ''accessory'' to the bond.—'''Accessory valves,''' in}}
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|''Pholas chiloensis,'' showing Accessory Valves (''a a'').}}}}
{{smaller|''zoöl.,'' small additional valves, as those placed near the umbones of the genus ''Pholas'' among mollusks.—'''Spinal accessory nerves,''' in ''anat.,'' the eleventh pair of cranial nerves. See ''accessorius.''}}

'''II.''' ''n.''; pl. ''accessories'' (-riz). '''1.''' In ''law,'' one who is guilty of a felony, not by committing the offense in person or as a principal, nor by being present at its commission, but by being in some other way concerned therein, as by advising or inciting another to commit the crime,

readings.c2s2:
or by concealing the offender or in any way helping him to escape punishment. {{smaller|An ''accessory before the fact'' is one who counsels or incites another to commit a felony, and who is not present when the act is done; ''after the fact'', one who receives and conceals, or in any way assists, the offender, knowing him to have committed a felony. In high treason and misdemeanor, by English law, there are no accessories, all implicated being treated as principals. See ''abetter''.}}
{{smaller block|An ''accessory'' is one who participates in a felony too remotely to be deemed a principal.
{{right|''Bishop''.}}}}
{{smaller block|In that state [Massachusetts], too, the aider and abettor, who at common law would have been but a mere ''accessory'', may be indicted and convicted of a substantive felony, without any regard to the indictment or conviction of the principal.
{{right|''Am. Cyc.'', I. 58.}}}}
{{smaller block|The prevailing rule of the criminal law, that there may be principals and ''accessories'' to a crime, has no application whatever to treason.
{{right|''Am. Cyc.'', XV. 851.}}}}

'''2.''' That which accedes or belongs to something else as its principal; a subordinate part or object; an accompaniment.
{{smaller block|The wealth of both Indies seems in great part but an ''accessary'' to the command of the sea.
{{right|''Bacon'', Essays, xxix.}}}}
{{smaller block|The aspect and ''accessories'' of a den of banditti.
{{right|''Carlyle''.}}}}

'''3.''' In the ''fine arts'', an object represented which is not a main motive or center of interest, but is introduced to balance the composition or in some way enhance its artistic effectiveness. {{smaller|In a portrait, for example, everything but the figure is an ''accessory''.}}
{{smaller block|In painting the picture of an Oriental, the pipe and the coffee-cup are indispensable ''accessories''.
{{right|''B. Taylor'', Lands of the Saracen, p. 178.}}}}

